        {
          "slug": "why-is-the-derivative-of-the-area-function-the-height",
          "title": "Why is the derivative of the area function the height?",
          "tag": "WHY",
          "summary": "Adds a thin strip of width h; the area grows by about f(x)·h (spec below)",
          "sections": "St 4.3",
          "extension": false,
          "spec": "Area function: drag x and shade A(x) = ∫ₐˣ f; plot A beside f. Strip: add width h; the new sliver is about a rectangle of height f(x), so ΔA ≈ f(x)·h. Limit: (A(x + h) − A(x))/h shown converging to f(x) as h shrinks. Consequence: ∫ₐᵇ f = F(b) − F(a) for any antiderivative F (FTC 2), with an odometer analogy.",
          "order": 1,
          "aids": []
        },
        {
